You want to... Write Why read a cheap value (<code>int</code>, <code>double</code>, small struct) <code>void f(int x)</code> copying a few bytes is the fastest thing there is read an expensive object <code>void f(const std::string&amp; s)</code> no copy, can't modify modify the caller's object <code>void f(std::string&amp; s)</code> caller sees the change <p>(There's a fourth — <code>T&amp;&amp;</code> — for "I want to steal from a temporary". That's Week 5's move semantics.)</p> examples/week02/lesson03.cpp<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;
#include &lt;vector&gt;

double average(const std::vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; v) {   // read-only, no copy
    if (v.empty()) return 0.0;
    long long sum = 0;
    for (int x : v) sum += x;
    return static_cast&lt;double&gt;(sum) / static_cast&lt;double&gt;(v.size());
}

void normalize(std::vector&lt;int&gt;&amp; v, int maxValue) {  // modifies the caller's vector
    for (int&amp; x : v) x = x &gt; maxValue ? maxValue : x;
}

void print_twice(const std::string&amp; s) { std::cout &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; s &lt;&lt; '\n'; }

int main() {
    int volume = 5;
    int&amp; alias = volume;
    alias = 7;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "volume=" &lt;&lt; volume &lt;&lt; '\n';

    std::vector&lt;int&gt; levels{3, 9, 12, 4};
    normalize(levels, 10);
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "average=" &lt;&lt; average(levels) &lt;&lt; '\n';

    print_twice(std::string{"temp"});   // const&amp; binds to a temporary

    const std::string&amp; ref = levels.size() &gt; 2 ? std::string{"many"} : std::string{"few"};
    auto copy = ref;            // auto drops the reference: std::string copy
    const auto&amp; same = ref;     // keeps the reference
    std::cout &lt;&lt; copy &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; same &lt;&lt; ' ' &lt;&lt; std::boolalpha &lt;&lt; (&amp;same == &amp;ref) &lt;&lt; '\n';
}
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>volume=7
average=6.5
temp temp
many many true
</code></pre>

Storage duration Examples Lives... automatic local variables, parameters from its definition until the end of its block static globals, <code>static</code> locals, <code>static</code> members for the whole program (locals: from first use) thread <code>thread_local</code> variables for the lifetime of the thread (Week 13) dynamic <code>new</code>/<code>delete</code>, owned by smart pointers until you (or a smart pointer) free it <p>Automatic objects are destroyed in reverse order of construction when their block ends. That's deterministic, and it's the foundation of RAII (Week 4). Let's watch it happen:</p> examples/week02/lesson04.cpp<pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;
#include &lt;string&gt;

struct Noisy {
    std::string name;
    explicit Noisy(std::string n) : name(std::move(n)) { std::cout &lt;&lt; "  construct " &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; '\n'; }
    ~Noisy() { std::cout &lt;&lt; "  destroy   " &lt;&lt; name &lt;&lt; '\n'; }
};

namespace media::audio {           // C++17 nested namespace
int counter() {
    static Noisy once{"static-local"};  // constructed on first call only
    static int calls = 0;
    return ++calls;
}
}  // namespace media::audio

Noisy global{"global"};            // static storage: before main, destroyed after main

int main() {
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "main starts\n";
    Noisy outer{"outer"};
    {
        Noisy inner{"inner"};
        const int n = media::audio::counter();   // first call constructs the static
        std::cout &lt;&lt; "  calls=" &lt;&lt; n &lt;&lt; '\n';
    }   // inner destroyed here
    using media::audio::counter;
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "  calls=" &lt;&lt; counter() &lt;&lt; '\n';
    std::cout &lt;&lt; "main ends\n";
}   // outer destroyed here; then static-local, then global (reverse order)
</code></pre> <p>Output:</p> <pre><code>  construct global
main starts
  construct outer
  construct inner
  construct static-local
  calls=1
  destroy   inner
  calls=2
main ends
  destroy   outer
  destroy   static-local
  destroy   global
</code></pre> <p>Look at the order: <code>outer</code> constructed, then <code>inner</code>; at the closing brace <code>inner</code> dies first. The function-local <code>static</code> is constructed the first time <code>counter()</code> runs and destroyed after <code>main</code> returns.</p>
